# Superseded work from before the microservices rewrite -- 2,626 files of an
# earlier pipeline that nothing imports. Kept on disk for reference, kept out of
# the repository because it is 96% of everything untracked here and would bury
# the pipeline that is actually in use.
_archive_pre_microservices/

# ...but the trained checkpoints ARE committed, deliberately.
#
# `*.pt` above still excludes experimental checkpoints, which is what that rule
# is for. These three are the shipped artefacts, and without them a clone cannot
# run at all:
#
#   ghosttrace_gnn.pt        loaded by ghosttrace/inference_server.py at startup.
#                            Absent, the server exits and the backend silently
#                            degrades to its rule-based fallback.
#   ghosttrace_gnn_best.pt   the bare state_dict for the same model.
#   encoder_pretrained.pt    the self-supervised encoder. Needed to reproduce
#                            training with `train.py --init-encoder`; without it
#                            that flag exits rather than quietly training from
#                            scratch and producing a different model.
#
# ~1.1MB for all three, against ~40 minutes of retraining for anyone who clones.
#
# A negation cannot re-include them here, because `*.pt` matches the files
# directly and the more specific rule would still need to come after -- simpler
# and less fragile to add them once with `git add -f`. Since .gitignore does not
# apply to tracked files, retraining shows up as an ordinary modification:
#
#   git add -f ghosttrace/ghosttrace_gnn.pt #              ghosttrace/ghosttrace_gnn_best.pt #              ghosttrace/encoder_pretrained.pt
